<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
	The repository add-on. It ships no code: its whole content is the four URLs
	below, which tell Kodi where to fetch the index, how to verify it, and where
	the add-on archives live.

	The element and attribute set here was checked against Kodi rather than
	recalled. Two sources, both read on 2026-08-23:

	  - https://kodi.wiki/view/Add-on_repositories (marked "updated for v19")
	  - Kodi's own Omega branch: xbmc/addons/Repository.cpp
	    (ParseDirConfiguration, FetchIndex, ResolvePathAndHash),
	    xbmc/addons/addoninfo/AddonInfoBuilder.cpp and
	    addons/repository.xbmc.org/addon.xml, which is Team Kodi's own
	    repository add-on as it ships in Omega.

	Four details are worth stating, because a manifest written from memory gets
	each of them wrong:

	  - <dir> is mandatory. The flat form, with <info> and friends directly under
	    the extension element, was removed in Kodi 20; Omega's constructor logs an
	    error and serves nothing.
	  - There is no compressed="..." attribute and no zip="..." attribute here.
	    Both appear in the wiki's examples and neither is read by
	    ParseDirConfiguration. compressed applied to Kodi 15 and earlier; zip was
	    dropped in v17.
	  - checksum carries verify="sha256". Without that attribute Kodi fetches the
	    checksum file, uses it only to notice that addons.xml changed, and never
	    verifies anything - the wiki says so in as many words. With it, FetchIndex
	    digests the response and refuses a mismatch.
	  - <hashes>sha256</hashes>, not "true". "true" is a deprecated alias for md5,
	    and Kodi logs md5 as broken. Kodi asks for a content-sha256 header first
	    and falls back to <archive-url>.sha256, which is the file the generator
	    writes beside every archive, because GitHub Pages sends no such header.

	Every URL below is derived by tools/build_repo.py into the tree it publishes,
	so this file is the one place they are written. Changing one moves the file
	the generator writes, and tests/test_build_repo.py asserts the two agree.
-->
<addon id="repository.onedrive.kn" name="OneDrive KN Repository" version="1.0.0" provider-name="Kenny Nguyen">
	<requires>
		<import addon="xbmc.addon" version="12.0.0" />
	</requires>
	<extension point="xbmc.addon.repository">
		<dir>
			<info>https://anhyeuviolet.github.io/kodi.plugin.onedrive/repo/addons.xml</info>
			<checksum verify="sha256">https://anhyeuviolet.github.io/kodi.plugin.onedrive/repo/addons.xml.sha256</checksum>
			<datadir>https://anhyeuviolet.github.io/kodi.plugin.onedrive/repo</datadir>
			<hashes>sha256</hashes>
		</dir>
	</extension>
	<extension point="xbmc.addon.metadata">
		<platform>all</platform>
		<summary lang="en_GB">Updates for OneDrive KN, over the network</summary>
		<description lang="en_GB">
Where Kodi looks for new versions of the OneDrive KN add-on.

Installing this once is the last time a file has to be carried to the television by hand. Afterwards
Kodi checks this location on its own schedule and offers, or installs, whatever is newer.

It contains no media, no accounts and no code of its own - only the address of the published files
and the checksums that say the download arrived intact.
		</description>
		<license>GPL-3.0-or-later</license>
		<source>https://github.com/anhyeuviolet/kodi.plugin.onedrive</source>
		<forum>https://github.com/anhyeuviolet/kodi.plugin.onedrive/issues</forum>
		<assets>
			<icon>icon.png</icon>
		</assets>
		<news>
v1.0.0:
- First release. Points Kodi at the published OneDrive KN files over HTTPS, with SHA-256 verification of the index and of every archive.
		</news>
	</extension>
</addon>
